## System Calls Used

The program uses only low-level system calls to interact with the operating system. Below is a description of each system call used, why it is used, where it appears in the code, and how errors are handled.

fgets()  
Used to read a full line of input from the user.  
Appears in the main loop to get each command line.  
If it returns NULL (e.g. Ctrl+D), the shell exits.

strtok()  
Used to split the user input into tokens (command + arguments), based on spaces or tabs.  
This is essential for separating the command from its parameters.  
It is used inside a helper function called parse_input().

getenv()  
Used to retrieve the user's $HOME environment variable.  
This is required when:  
- The user types cd with no arguments  
- Searching for a command in the $HOME directory  
The return value is always checked to ensure it is not NULL.

chdir()  
Used to change the current working directory of the shell process.  
This is called when the user runs the cd command.  
If the directory change fails (e.g. folder does not exist), the program prints an error using perror().

access()  
Checks whether a file exists and is executable (X_OK).  
It is used when searching for an external command in $HOME and /bin.  
If access fails, the shell continues to the next location or prints an error if not found.

fork()  
Used to create a child process so the external command can run in a separate process.  
It is used inside the function that executes external commands.  
If fork fails (returns -1), the shell prints an error with perror() and skips execution.

execv()  
Replaces the child process with the external command.  
Called only inside the child process (after fork).  
If execv fails (e.g. file is not a valid executable), the child prints the error using perror() and exits.

waitpid()  
The parent process (the shell) uses this to wait for the child process (the command) to finish.  
This ensures that the shell doesn't continue before the command finishes running.  
After the child ends, the shell prints the return code using WEXITSTATUS().

perror()  
Used to print a human-readable error message when a system call fails.  
For example:  
- If cd fails (invalid path)  
- If execv fails (not executable)  
The error is printed immediately to help the user understand what went wrong.

## Program Flow Summary

- Display prompt
- Read input using fgets
- Parse input using strtok
- If command is "exit" → exit program
- If command is "cd" → change directory using chdir (with fallback to HOME)
- If external:
   - Check $HOME/command using access()
   - If not found, check /bin/command
   - If found:
       - fork()
       - In child: execv()
       - In parent: waitpid()
   - If not found or not executable: print error
- Repeat

## Efficiency

- No dynamic memory allocation (no malloc)
- Input and arguments are stored in fixed-size arrays
- Parsing is done in-place (no copies)
- No unnecessary processing

## Error Handling

- All system calls are checked for errors
- perror() is used to print detailed system messages
- If a command is not found in $HOME or /bin, a clear message is printed
- If cd fails (e.g. directory doesn't exist), perror() is used

